!panic: a signal handler used this runtime's record of the buffers lent to C while the code it interrupted was inside it, which would corrupt memory: a Heroes function given to C as a signal handler, or called from one, does so whenever it lends a buffer or a local to C through `@`. No function of the program is a signal handler, nor called by one: after `hero_net_catch_stop() -> i64` (`hero_net.h`), Ctrl-C and SIGTERM only make `hero_net_stop_asked() -> i64` answer 1 and a waiting call of that layer answer stopped; `hero_signal_watch(sig: i32) -> i64` (`hero_os.h`) counts any other signal, and `hero_signal_take(sig: i32) -> i64` gives the count since its last call
